Let $p$ represent the cost of a pencil and $q$ represent the cost of a pen. We are given the following equations based on the problem statement:

\begin{align*}
5p + q &= 2.50 \quad \text{(1)} \\
p + 2q &= 1.85 \quad \text{(2)}
\end{align*}

We want to find the cost of two pencils and one pen, which is represented by $2p + q$. To solve this, we need to find the values of $p$ and $q$.

First, we can multiply equation (2) by 5 to align the coefficients of $p$ with equation (1):

\begin{align*}
5(p + 2q) &= 5(1.85) \\
5p + 10q &= 9.25 \quad \text{(3)}
\end{align*}

Now, we can subtract equation (1) from equation (3):

\begin{align*}
(5p + 10q) - (5p + q) &= 9.25 - 2.50 \\
9q &= 6.75 \\
q &= \frac{6.75}{9} \\
q &= 0.75
\end{align*}

Now that we have the value of $q$, we can substitute it back into equation (2) to find $p$:

\begin{align*}
p + 2(0.75) &= 1.85 \\
p + 1.50 &= 1.85 \\
p &= 1.85 - 1.50 \\
p &= 0.35
\end{align*}

Finally, we can find the cost of two pencils and one pen:

\begin{align*}
2p + q &= 2(0.35) + 0.75 \\
&= 0.70 + 0.75 \\
&= \boxed{1.45}
\end{align*}
